.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CP 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm cp
.Nd copy files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Oo
.Fl R
.Op Fl H | L | P
.Oc
.Op Fl f | i | n
.Op Fl alpsvx
.Ar source_file target_file
.Nm
.Oo
.Fl R
.Op Fl H | L | P
.Oc
.Op Fl f | i | n
.Op Fl alpsvx
.Ar source_file ... target_directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
In the first form
.Nm
copies
.Ar source_file
to
.Ar target_file .
In the second, the last operand is an existing directory and each
.Ar source_file
is copied into it under its own name.
.Pp
An existing target file is overwritten in place, keeping its mode.
A new one gets the source's mode, less the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits
and the umask.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Archive: the same as
.Fl RpP .
.It Fl f
If an existing target can't be opened, remove it and try again.
.It Fl H
With
.Fl R ,
follow symbolic links named on the command line.
.It Fl i
Ask on the standard error before overwriting a file, reading the answer from
the standard input; anything but an answer beginning with
.Ql y
leaves the file as it is.
.It Fl L
With
.Fl R ,
follow every symbolic link.
.It Fl l
Make hard links to the sources instead of copies.
.It Fl n
Don't overwrite an existing file.
.It Fl P
With
.Fl R ,
follow no symbolic link (the default).
.It Fl p
Preserve each file's modification and access times, owner and group, and mode.
If the owner can't be kept, neither are the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits.
.It Fl R
Copy directories and everything in them.
Symbolic links are copied as links, and FIFOs and devices are made anew rather
than read from.
A new directory gets its source's mode less the umask.
.Fl r
is taken as
.Fl R .
.It Fl s
Make symbolic links to the sources instead of copies.
.It Fl v
Name each file as it is copied.
.It Fl x
Don't copy directories on other file systems than the one each operand is on.
.El
.Pp
Of
.Fl f ,
.Fl i
and
.Fl n ,
the last given counts, as does the last of
.Fl H ,
.Fl L
and
.Fl P .
Without
.Fl R ,
symbolic links are followed and what they point to is copied.
.Pp
A file is never copied onto itself, nor a directory into a copy of itself.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A file left alone by
.Fl n ,
or by answering no to
.Fl i ,
is not a failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a tree, keeping times and modes:
.Dl $ cp -Rp /usr/share/examples/etc ~/etc.orig
.Pp
Copy several files into a directory, asking before replacing any:
.Dl $ cp -i *.conf /etc/
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ln 1 ,
.Xr mv 1 ,
.Xr symlink 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
.Fl a ,
.Fl l ,
.Fl n ,
.Fl s ,
.Fl v
and
.Fl x
are extensions, as in the BSDs.
Unlike FreeBSD's,
.Nm
gives a source ending in
.Ql /
no special meaning: the directory itself is copied.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
